+In orbit (tests/navigation.test.ts) the filter's σ is the size of its errors through a 1 500 s
+coast without aiding (tactical, 200 draws: mean (error/σ)² per axis 0.8–1.2 for position,
+velocity and attitude), and with GNSS and the star tracker it tells the two parts of a bias
+apart: in 2 000 s its estimate of the turn-on part is within 0.15 °/h and 20 µg of it (of 1 °/h
+and 500 µg; 20 draws). GNSS alone cannot see a gyro's bias in free fall, where the attitude does
+not reach the velocity.
+
+**What it shows on Falcon 9 to LEO** (crosswind, seed 20260919; tests/navigation.test.ts). A
+flight is one draw of the sensors' errors; the ranges (10–90 %) are of 30 draws flown through the
+same flight's truth.
+
+- Tactical grade with GNSS: position within ±3σ through staging (mean normalised error 1.2 per
+  axis, no sample outside 3σ; over the draws 1.0 and 0.1 %), under 5 m and 0.1 m/s; the orbit it
+  believes in is the true one to 270 m in apoapsis and 5 m in periapsis. Its gyro noise
+  (0.008 °/s per axis at 100 Hz) reaches the rate loop, and the jets' deadband below keeps it off
+  them.
+- A GNSS outage from T+60 s to T+200 s: the error grows to 42 m and 0.4 m/s in this flight
+  (43–202 m and 0.6–3.5 m/s over the draws, RMS 124 m and 2.1 m/s, as the filter's σ of 129 m and
+  2.1 m/s says), and falls back to metres at the first fix.
+- MEMS without GNSS: 8.6 km and 30 m/s of error at SECO (5.4–15 km); guidance cuts off on an
+  orbit the navigation believes is 197 × 490 km while the true one is 199 × 525 km. The star
+  tracker, above 150 km, brings the attitude error from 1.4° to seconds of arc.
+- Tactical without GNSS: 1.0 km and 3.5 m/s at SECO (0.56–1.6 km); the apoapsis 2.6 km off.
+- Navigation grade without GNSS: 70 m and 0.3 m/s at SECO (40–160 m); the apoapsis within 0.1 km.
+
+Held for the flight, the turn-on biases make the unaided errors at SECO larger than when they
+faded with the 300 s (medians over the draws: MEMS 7.8 to 8.3 km and 23 to 37 m/s, tactical 0.81
+to 1.0 km and 2.8 to 3.8 m/s, navigation grade 75 to 80 m and 0.28 to 0.34 m/s); the GNSS-aided
+figures and the outage are as before (mean normalised error 0.93 and 1.0, outage RMS 125 and
+124 m).
+
+A limit: a MEMS unit with GNSS but without the star tracker drifts in attitude at its 30 °/h
+through a coast, to 8° in 1 000 s and 25° in 3 000 s, beyond the small-angle error model, and the
+noise of the measured specific force in −[f×]φ reads as attitude information: at 400 km the
+filter's attitude σ falls below its error (mean (error/σ)² per axis about 2 at 1 000 s, 20–30 at
+3 000 s; 20 draws). With the star tracker, on by default above 150 km, or a tactical or
+navigation-grade unit it stays consistent.
 
